n  executed then you can have jenkins run  you can schedule build to run at a  specified time so you may have nightly  builds of your code that illustrates the  changes in the solution you're building  and then finally you also can manually  build a environment on request  occasionally you will want to also  restart jenkins and so it's good that  when a question around how do you  restart jenkins manually comes up that  you have the answers and there are two  ways in which you can do it one is you  can force a restart without waiting for  bills to complete by using the jenkins  url that you have for your environment  slash restart or you can allow all  running bills to complete before we  start are required in which case you  would use the command of the url for  your jenkins environment slash safe  restart so before we talk about selenium  let's understand a few drawbacks of  manual testing let's take an example of  a very simple use case as a user i want  to login to my web application using  username and password a very simple use  case and it's a very common use case for  any kind of web application you're  working so as a tester what do i do i  need to write multiple test case to test  this use case so i can use multiple data  sets like valid credentials invalid  credentials null values and so on to  test this particular use case and then  what you do you start executing the test  cases one by one you find bugs then your  developers fixes them and then you will  need to rerun all the test cases again  and again until all the bugs of for this  use case are fixed and your application  is ready to ship now look into this now  this brings us to the first merit  demerit of the manual testing which is  what if one has to run test cases with  hundred transactions and repeat them  again and again this is going to be a  very time consuming process and very  boring also it is more error prone since  it's done manually and human mistakes  are bound to happen the third cha